# Vendored subtrees. Marking them generated collapses them by default in
# GitHub diffs and excludes them from language statistics, so review focuses
# on Qudo-authored files. Content is upstream; review the pinned SHA, not the
# files. See docs/branching.md.
openssl/**          linguist-generated=true
openssl/**          linguist-vendored=true
qudo-pqc-lib/**     linguist-generated=true
qudo-pqc-lib/**     linguist-vendored=true

# Keep line endings sane for the Windows build path.
*.ps1  text eol=crlf
*.sh   text eol=lf

# Binary test fixtures must never be line-ending converted.
#
# Upstream OpenSSL carries these rules in its own .gitattributes, but that file
# never reached the vendored subtree: upstream marks `.*` as export-ignore, so
# `git archive` drops every dotfile when the subtree is taken. Without the
# rules, a Windows checkout (Git for Windows defaults to core.autocrlf=true)
# rewrites LF to CRLF inside the fixtures and every digest taken over them is
# wrong — openssl/test/data.bin grows 128 -> 132 bytes, and the dgst, HMAC and
# XOF recipes in test_dgst fail with values that look like a crypto fault.
#
# Measured: SHAKE-128 xoflen=64 over test/data.bin is
#   bb565dac...8e4677  with LF   (what the test expects)
#   71172feb...131b647 with CRLF (what Windows CI reported)
*.bin  binary
*.der  binary
*.pfx  binary
*.dat  binary
openssl/fuzz/corpora/**  binary
